## The line

TIES is dead simple on purpose. TIES labels, timestamps and chains hashes. Nothing more.
Anything more complicated (schemas, field layouts, keys, Merkle leaves, selective disclosure, policy) is up to the
operator or the institution, to venture into as they see fit. TIES does not require it, check it or store it.

## The vision: agents working with people

A person gathers and organizes things in their own way. It makes sense to them; it will not make sense to another
person or another language model. An agent alone codifies a schema with little basis and nothing durable.

TIES joins the two. The catalog states the abstract meaning once, in plain words, at a fixed URL. The person keeps
the concrete content. The agent helps shape and hash it. Anyone (the person on a phone, a colleague, an institution,
a different agent) can read what was done and when from the labels, without seeing the content.

What is unique: the hash is labeled. "An answer to this question existed at this time" becomes knowable, and the
answer stays private. The DAG is a labeled tool, not a mystery.

## The three powers (SICP 1.1)

Every powerful language has three: primitive expressions, means of combination, means of abstraction.
TIES gives all three to the human and to the agent alike. Each uses their own tools; the calls are the same.

1. primitive: name a new thing, with its definition (a catalog entry, or a private one in your own register).
2. combine / decompose: tie parts into a whole (one entry, several parents), or take a whole back into its parts.
3. abstract: name a combination so it can be used as one unit.

The balance is in who can create, not in what has been created. Every entry says who made it: human, agent, or both.

## The canonical register

One file, append-only, one JSON line per entry. Fields:

    n        line number, from 1
    when     ISO 8601 with offset
    who      the federated id of the holder (the constant)
    label    a catalog URL: the abstract meaning, public
    inside   sha256:<64 hex> of the exact bytes of the content, private
    parents  list of sha256 of earlier entries this one ties to (empty for the first)
    memo     public memo; outside the content, never changes the inside hash
    by       human | agent | both
    prev     hash of the line before (null on line 1)
    hash     sha256 of the compact line without "hash"

Exact bytes: the bytes as stored, no normalization. A trailing newline counts.
A folder: inside = sha256 of its listing, one line per file "<sha256>  <path>", sorted by path.
The label and the inside hash are in the same hashed line, so content cannot be relabeled.
Modify = a new entry whose parents include the old one. Nothing is rewritten.

Hash the combined content, not single fields. "government name: john doe" alone is guessable; the whole
registration in your own words is not. Short answers (yes, a date, a city) go inside a larger block or get a random nonce.
Per-field hashes, if wanted, stay in your own register.

Your data stays yours: a git repository or anywhere else. Git is already a content-addressed DAG.

## The calls (5)

    GET  /                  what TIES is, in plain words
    GET  /catalog           the catalog: every label, its URL and its hash
    GET  /catalog/<entry>   one label: the exact bytes and sha256
    POST /receipt           an entry line -> a timestamped receipt; stores nothing
    POST /verify            a receipt -> valid or not

Registration is the first receipt, under the registration label. his word: [[ registration as the first /receipt, or its own POST /register ]]
All five are live on tiesapi.com. A browser gets a page; curl gets JSON.
Agents: the same calls, also offered as an MCP server (a layer over the calls, not a sixth call).

## The steps

1. Gather. The human gathers what seems related, in their own way. Nothing is required yet.
2. Register. The holder registers once (identity, institution, roles, worldline) as one combined block; the hash goes
   on line 1 under the registration label. The holder is the constant.
3. Label. Pick the catalog URL for what is being done (a prompt, an SOP step). Fetch it; check its hash.
4. Answer. The human answers in their own words, voice, photo, anything. The agent helps separate and combine.
5. Hash. sha256 of the exact bytes. That is the inside.
6. Enter. One line in the register: label, inside, parents, memo, by.
7. Receipt. POST the line to tiesapi.com/receipt. Keep the receipt with the line.
8. Keep. Commit the content and the register to your own repository.
9. Use the powers. A new primitive, a combination, a decomposition or an abstraction is just another entry.
10. Verify. Anyone holding a line and its receipt can POST /verify. The register checks itself: every prev and hash recomputes.

An SOP run is steps 3 to 7 once per SOP step, each entry's parent the step before. Checking a run: every step label is
present, in order, each with an inside hash. No content is exposed.

